## Introduction
Thermally activated delayed fluorescent (TADF) materials, owing to its highly effective utilization of triple-state excitons, has become a rapidly-developing field owing to their highly potential application in organic light-emitting diodes (OLEDs). The occurrence of TADF requires minimal energy splitting $(\Delta E s\tau)$ between the singlet (S) and triplet (T) excited states facilitate the reverse intersystem crossover (RlsC)， which is commonly achieved with donor-acceptor (D-A) systems that showing charge-transfer based emissions. The spatial separation of the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) of the donor and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO）of the acceptor is necessary to narrow the $\Delta E_{s T,}$ which requires the ingenious design of D-A system in both structural and orbital energy level perspectives. Accordingly, D-A molecules, based on covalent bonding connected D and A moieties, featuring twisted intramolecular charge transfer (TiCT） have been widely investigated as TADF materials.[1l On the other hand, through space charge transfer (TSCT), as an alternating approach to realize TADF, has attracted extremely attention recently.[2] Compared with the TiCT-TADFmaterials that requires complicated organic design and synthesis, the achievement of TSCT-TADF is highly dependent on the proper spatial arrangements of D and A components, through which the overlap of the HOMO and LUMO could be tuned in flexible manner to further narrow the $\Delta E_{s T}$ [3] In this way, TADF properties could be introduced into exciplexes, polymers， organic cocrystals， and metal-organic materials.[2d, 4] On the other hand, for the targeted modulation of TADF material, heavy atom effect (HAE) has been widely explored in previous research.[5] It is of great help to increase the rate constants of reverse intersystem crossover (KRIsc),which is one of the effective channels to promote the TADF of material. More importantly, heavy atoms (including halogen atoms F, Cl, Br, and I) have the potential to burgeon the probability of spin-orbital coupling (SOC） according to the perturbation theory.[5b, 6] Accompany with the enhancement of SOC that could facilitate TADF, the introducing of halogen could also be utilized to tune the electronegativity of component for excited state regulation.[7] Recently, Duan etc. reported that halogenation at the A unit will weaken the electron-donating property, revealing that addition of suitable halogen atoms as a feasible strategy to induce the hypsochromic shift of the emission spectra, which is desired for the fabrication of blue-emission OLED.[8]
The emission properties of metal-organic framework (MOF) have been reported to be highly tunable based on components tuning.[9] Therefore, in consideration of the potential of crystalline materials for the formation of multifarious D-A systems,[10] we have focused on the rational construction and functions investigation of D-A MOF systems.[11] Recently, we have demonstrated that a D-A MOF system, triphenylene (Tpl) @NKU111,could also be a highly potential TSCT-TADF platform by taking the advantages of the crystalline framework for the rational spatial alignment manipulation of D and A components.[12] Moreover, the highly tunable component and structure features of D-A MOF make it an accessible platform for the illustration of the determining factors of TSCT originated properties and the mechanism behind.[12-13] In addition, the highly crystalline nature and modulable morphology of MOF have also make it an ideal material for advanced optical applications in photonic integrated circuit (PlC)field. PlCs has been regarded as the critical elements in realizing communications, quantum information processing, nonlinear optics. In this regard, organic flexible crystals have been widely investigated owing to their versatility in components, morphology, and properties for the construction of organic PICs using mechanophotonics approach.[14] Solid foundation has been laid in the organic optical waveguide researches about the waveguide mechanisms (active/passive) and the expected
attributes of waveguide[15]. Accordingly, fundamental signal modulation and complicated optical functions could be achieved through the manipulation of light using flexible crystal circuits.[16] For the utilization of D-A MOFs in PlCs, we have shown that the MOF crystal could serve as waveguide for the propagation of light, and the rational integration of MOFs crystals featuring distinctive emissions could be utilized to realize complicated light signal modulation.[11c] By taking the advantage, the D-A MOFs could be developed as a unified platform for the realization of PlC functions targeted material design and crystal integration.
Herein, aiming at the further exploration of the library of D-A MOF based TADF materials and their advanced optical applications, we report the investigation of a new series of D-A MOF system that featuring highly tunable TSCT-TADF emission properties and their application for the construction of highly adjustable photonic modules. N-phenylcarbazole $(P h\mathbf{\cdot}C z)$ and derivatives have been introduced into the NKU-111 framework as guest to produce a new series of D-A MOF (Figure 1a). The configuration of the guest $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ donor was found to be regulated due to the confined space of the host cage in NKU-111,which promote its stacking and interaction with the nearly planar 2,4,6- tri(pyridin-4-yl)-1,3,5-triazine (TPT) acceptor to reveal TSCTTADF emission. Several -Br groups functionalized derivatives of $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ were also investigated to illustrate the impact of the HAE to the TSCT-TADF. The results show that the emission wavelength of the D-A MOF could be well tuned depending on the positions and numbers of the -Br groups, and the emission maximum could be modulated from 540 to $600\mathsf{n m}$ .Moreover, the -Br functionalization of Ph- $\scriptstyle.\mathsf{C}z$ could sharply increase the photoluminescence quantum yield(PLQY）(from $5.02\%$ to $30.86\%$ and $K_{\mathsf{R I S C}}$ (from $1.02\times10^{7}$ to $11.72\times10^{7}5^{-1})$ of the D-A MOF (Figure 1b and 1c). Furthermore, rod-like heterostructure crystals were rationally fabricated by integrating the D-A MOFs with distinctive TADF emissions, which could reveal excitation position dependent tip emissions from 495 to 598 nm. The highly adjustable emission of the heterostructure crystals make it potential to serve as photonic modules in PlCs applications.
## Results and Discussion
The NKU-111 has been developed and investigated as an ideal candidate for the construction of crystalline D-A system. The confined cage space composed of electron-deficient TPT moiety could be utilized to encapsulate various planar polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAH) as electron-rich donor to produce D-A systems with typical TSCT based emissions. The selection of donor was inspired by related traditional TiCT-TADF research, in which $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ and derivatives have been widely used as donor to construct D-A systems with different acceptors featuring 1,3,5- triazine moieties.[1b, 17] Comparing with the planar PAHs, the nonplanar configuration of $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ (Figure 1a), which is commonly observed in crystalline states and featuring a large torsion angle, shall not fit the dimension of confined cage space of NKU-111 well. To our surprise, the $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ was found to show configuration with a minor torsion angle to fit the confined cage space of NKU-111, indicating the potential of cage structure for guest molecules configuration regulation. More detail will be discussed in the following section. In combination with the phenomenon of increasing SOC by HAE,we chose to functionalize the carbazole with -Br groups,which is expected to optimize the TSCT-TADF in this host-guest system. Accordingly, several -Br functionalized Ph-Cz, namely Ph-Cz-Br(2), Ph-Cz-Br(3), Ph-Cz-2Br(2,7) and Ph-Cz-2Br(3,6) were investigated (Figure 1a).
The construction of targeted MOF is performed through the coordination directed assembly of guest, TPT, linker, and metal ion under solvothermal condition (see supporting information for detail), and highly crystalline product is obtained (Figure 1c), which presented similar rod-like morphology but distinctive color. The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Figure S1) and the unit cell parameters (Table S1) of the products matched that of the NKU-111 well, indicating the formation of the electrondeficient framework as expected. The successful encapsulation of the donor guests could be well characterized through the single crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) and $^1\mathsf{H}$ liquid NMR tests of the digested crystal samples (Figure S2 and S3-S7). Then the samples are named according to the components as PhCz@NKU-111(C1),Ph-Cz-Br(2)@NKU-111(C2)，Ph-Cz2Br(2,7)@NKU-111(C3), Ph-Cz-Br(3)@NKU-111(C4), and PhCz-2Br(3,6)@NKU-111(C5), respectively. Thermogravimetric (Figure S8) analysis ilustrated that these five compounds have remarkable thermal ability,which the decomposition temperature at around 362,364 369,363, $369^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for C1 to C5, respectively, which were higher than that of NKU-111 $(337^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ ,agree well with the formation of host-guest D-A MOF，which will reduce the encapsulation of solvent guest and enhance the stability of the framework through the strong D-A interaction. IR spectra of NKU111 and C1 to C5 (Figure S9) reveal similar adsorption peaks. From the comparison of IR spectra of pristine NKU-111, the guest molecules, and C1 to C5 (Figure S53-57), the conspicuous peaks around $700~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ could be attributed to the vibration of monosubstituted benzene ring on $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ ，indicating the successful incorporation of guest into the framework. The samples feature quite low apparent porosity according to the nitrogen adsorption isotherm obtained at $77~\mathsf{K}$ (Figure S10), in consideration to the well-preserved framework structure after gas sorption (Figure S11).
It should be noted that the triple-disordered $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ molecules could only be well modeled in part of the cages,while for the cages located in at the axial position, the $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ could be well modeled owing to the more complicated disordered state caused by the symmetry. Then $^1\mathsf{H}$ liquid NMR tests of the digested crystal samples were utilized to determine the content of guest and verify the integrity of the host framework, which could also be ensured the high loading of $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ in NKU-111. The spectrum of the digested C1 (guest feeding amount with $0.025~\mathrm{mmol};$ ）revealthe signal of TPT, PTA, and $\mathsf{P h{-}C z}$ ，indicating the presence of $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ in the framework. The experimental mole ratio of 3:1.98 (PTA : TPT) fit the theoretical value of 3:2 (based on NKU-111, $\{[\mathsf{C d}_{3}(\mathsf{T P T})_{2}(\mathsf{P T A})_{3}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3}]\cdot2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\}_{\mathsf{n}})$ well (Figure S12 calculation method was presented in supporting information), indicating the structural integrity of the framework. Accordingly, the ratio of Ph$\mathtt{C z}$ loading in the framework was calculated to be $85.05\%$ (Figure S3). Similar results were obtained with the samples of C2 to C5, showing that the $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ and derivatives could be encapsulated well into the framework of NKU-111 without the triggering of
structural defection (Figure S12-S16).
In addition, since the photophysical properties of compounds were commonly performed at room temperature (RT), the configuration of $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ in NKU-111 at RT was investigated for better understanding of the correlation between the structure features and properties. The $\mathsf{\Omega}^{13}\mathsf{C}$ solid-state NMR test was performed at RT with bulk sample to determine the configuration of the guest in the framework, as the $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ could not be well modeled from SCxRD at RT due to the stronger thermal vibration. As shown in Figure 2b, two broad peaks were found in the $\mathsf{\Omega}^{13}\mathsf{C}$ solid-state NMR spectrum of C1 at RT,which could be assigned to the seven magnetically inequivalent carbons of $\mathsf{P h\mathrm{-}C z}$ that are connected to hydrogens.However, the observed chemical shift range of 132-118 ppm for these carbons is inconsistent with the range (130.0-109.9 ppm) reported in the $\mathsf{\Omega}^{13}\mathsf{C}$ liquid NMR test.[18] Then the details of Ph-Cz configuration can be uncovered by theoretical modelling of $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ chemical shifts. Specifically, the dihedral angle (or the torsion angle)between $\mathsf{P h}$ and $\mathsf{C}z$ is allowed to vary from $0^{\circ}$ to $90^{\circ}$ with an increment of $10^{\circ}$ The chemical shifts of aforementioned seven carbons are then calculated and compared with the values observed in the $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ solid-state NMR tests (Figure 2b and Figure S17). The determination of the configuration of the guest molecules at RT is based on the analysis of the characteristic peak of the C atom at the 1 and 8-positions of the $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ backbone, which is very sensitive to the torsion angle between Ph and $\mathtt{C}z$ moieties.The chemical shift of this peak significantly increases when the torsion angle decreases whereas the chemical shifts of other peaks are always within 120-130 ppm. This peak finally merges with other peaks to give rise to two broad features when $\mathsf{P h}$ and $\mathsf{C}z$ is less than $10^{\circ}$ . Then the absence of the signal below ${\sim}120$ ppm in the experimental spectrum strongly supports that the configuration of Ph-Cz molecule adapt a reduced torsion angle to fit the cage at RT. All these results indicate that the configuration of the $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ molecules has been regulated by the confined cage space of the host framework for fitting (Figure 2a).
Based on the well-determined confined configuration of $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ in NKU-111 at RT, the potential impact of donor configuration to the emission properties is evaluated and compared with that of organic based TSCT-TADF systems (Table S3). We have processed the density functional theory (DFT) calculation of the HOMO and LUMO of Ph-Cz featuring different configurations, i.e., planar, and optimized free configuration (angle between Ph and $\mathsf{C}z$ planes at $53.7^{\circ}.$ ),respectively (Figure S18).The relative tiny difference between the HOMO level $(0.059\mathsf{e V})$ of the two states of Ph-Cz as donor indicate the potential of realizing similar TSCTTADF when interact with the same triazine acceptor. On the other hand, the nearly planar configuration of $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ may result in shorter distance between D and A moiety and enhance the D-A interaction. Compared with the molecular based TADF materials which is commonly investigated in solution or film state, the energy levels in the crystalline solid are degenerated by the surrounding structure, which results in the broadening of the absorption energy level and the reduction of the gap between the HOMO and LUMO.[19] Therefore, the crystalline state of the MOFs may play a significant role on their photophysical properties.
The UV-vis spectra displayed the noticeable absorption peak around $370{-}560\ \mathrm{nm}$ for C1 to C5 compared with NKU-111,which indicate the formation of D-A system featuring CT behavior (Figure S19).[20] The compared emission spectra between the ligands, guest, and these compounds show that the emission of compounds is different from that of both the guest and the ligands (Figure S23-S27). Photoluminescence (PL) spectra (Figure 3a) of C1-C5 show broad emission band with the emission maximums covered 540-600nm.ThecorrespondingCommission Internationale de I' Eclairage (ClE) coordinates of their emissions were calculated (Figure 3b), which covered green to red area and show quite high emission color purity. Specifically, the maximum PL emission wavelength is $592~\mathsf{n m}$ for C1, 569 nm for C2, and $543\mathsf{n m}$ for C3, indicating that the functionalization of donor with - Br will result in remarkable hypsochromic shift of the emission, and the effect is functionalization position dependent. Noticeable blue shift may be induced by the -Br group that could weaken the electron- donating characteristic of donor. Further, C4 and C5 showed the maximum emission spectra peaks at 588 nm and 584 nm respectively. As shown in Figure 3e, the tendency of the HOMO/LUMO gap between the donor and acceptor could roughly predict the emission of the MOFs and matched well with their experimental emission maximums, indicating the D-A charge transfer origin of the emissions.
The PL decay profile of C1-C3 were investigated at RT (Figure 3c) for better understanding of the emission process. C1 possessed a prompt fluorescence with $19.01$ ns $\left(\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{p}}\right)$ and a delayed fluorescence（FL） with $0.48\ensuremath{\upmu\leq}\left(\uptau_{\mathrm{d}}\right)$ . The prompt fluorescence with 8.02 ns $\left(\mathfrak{T}_{\mathfrak{p}}\right)$ and a delayed FL for 0.70 μs (Td) were observed for C2. In terms of C3, the prompt fluorescence with 7.36 ns $\left(\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{p}}\right)$ and the prompt fluorescence of 1.70 μs (Td). The noticeable decline of prompt fluorescence and marked proliferation of delayed FL with the increase of -Br were presented indicating that the -Br group were instrumental in curtailing the prompt fluorescence and faster the process of RlsC, which could promote the TADF. Additionally, the energy levels of $\mathsf{S}_{1}$ and ${\sf T}_{1}$ states of C1-C3 could be estimated by the fluorescence spectra at RT and the phosphorescence spectra at $77\mathsf{K}$ (Figure S28-S30) Compounds C1-C3 revealed small AEsr as 0.028, 0.01, 0.025 eV respectively, indicating that these three compounds habilitate the feasible condition for the happening of TADF.
Due to the unambiguous regularity, the in-depth measurement and properties investigations concentrated on C1-C3. The PLQY presents a sharply increase from $5.02\%$ for C1, $12.21\%$ for C2, to $30.86\%$ for C3. The result of enhancive PLQY should be attributed to the effective utilization of the triplet exciton energy origin from the TSCT-TADF,which is enhanced by the -Br groups induced
HAE. In addition, we reckoned the rate constants of intersystem crossover (Kisc) and the KRisc of C1-C3 based on the prompt and delayed lifetime for fluorescence and PLQY. In terms of relative calculation method[21] the related parameters and constants were concluded in Table 1.With the increasing of -Br groups, the ratio of delayed FL surged from $83.05\%$ to $99.50\%$ . Prominently incremental proportion of delayed FL with the rising amount of decorated -Br groups indicated the enhancive percentage of process for RlSC among C1 to C3, promoting the noticeable TSCT-TADF. On the other hand, the impact of temperature to the proportion of delayedlifetime was further verified. The temperature-dependent PL spectra and temperature-dependent PL delay curves were attained for C1-C3 (Figure 3d and S34 to S38). Through the comparison of the results, the -Br functionalization could increase the percentage of delayed FL with the enhancement of temperature, and the RlSC process might even overcome the non-radiation dissipation of triple-state excitons in C3 from $180\mathsf{K}$ to $200\mathsf{K}.$ From the above comparison and results, the TADF characteristic could be clearly proved in these compounds due to the delayed lifetime and temperaturedependent PL delay curves. The SOC infection due to the functionalization of -Br group of donors played a significant role on promoting the TADF,which obviously enhance the process of RISC.
In the structure of NKU-111, each triangular prism cages only have the space to accommodate one guest, then the loading proportion could easily be grasped through the feeding amount of guest in synthesis process. Herein, the compound C3，which reveal the most optimized photophysical properties of the five compounds, was selected to investigated the regulation of PLQY. The strategy aimed to fix the amount of TPT ( $\left\langle0.05\mathsf{m m o l}\right..$ andPTA $(0.1\ \mathrm{mmol})$ but to change the feeding amount of guest Ph-Cz2Br(2,7) from $0.5~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ to $150~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}$ .The consistency of pattern and structure for C3 was presented in PXRD (Figure S39). The UV-vis spectra of C3 with different guest feed amounts was obtained, presenting that the absorption band near $470\ \mathsf{n m}$ was greatly enhanced since intermolecular TSCT interactions after increasing the guest feed amount (Figure S40). PL spectra show the nearly identical PL emission, indicating that the different loading ratio could not affect the TSCT based emission (Figure S41). The $^1\mathsf{H}$ liquid NMR spectra of the digested compounds were implemented to determine the loading ratio of Ph-Cz-2Br(2,7) in the different samples (Figure S42). The ratio of loading guest in cages could be modulated from $7.55\%$ to $99.79\%$ due to the incremental feeding of guest. Simultaneously, PLQY for the series of samples burgeon from $12.31\%$ to $35.7\%$ , indicating the superior regulation of PLQY in this system (Table S4). Further, positive correlation was observed between PLQY and the loading amount of guest (Figure S43), showing PLQY of C3 would be improved by the incremental feeding amount of guest and proportion of guest loaded in cage.
The achievement of NKU-111 framework based TADF MOFs featuring enhanced PLQY could further expanded the library of functional crystal domains, which is highly desired for the realization and optimization of complex functions based on rationally integration of MOFs. Aiming at the fabrication of highly photonic modules based on TADF MOFs， the photonic propagation behavior of the crystals of C3 and C5 were characterized. PL images (Figure S44 and S47) and spectra (Figure S45 and S48) of C3 and C5 crystals by exciting with a $\overline{{405}}$ nm laser at different positions were performed. The characteristic emissions of the compounds could be detected at the tips of the crystals upon the excitation at different positions, indicating the positive waveguide behaviors, and the optical loss coefficients (R) of C3 and C5 are determined to be 0.0152 dB $\upmu\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ and 0.0355 ${\mathsf{d B}}\ {\mathsf{\upmu}}{\mathsf{m}}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure S46 and S49). These remarkable optical waveguide performances make them highly potential to realize the energy transfer (ET) and optical signal modulation based on heterostructure crystal.[16c-e]
For the realization of optical signal tuning with the photonic modules, the cascaded ET between heterostructure domains should be rationally designed refer to the contacted molecular crystals, in which the ET is achieved through the propagation of donor emission photons to the acceptor.[14b] The triphenylene (Tpl)@NKU-111 featuring TADF emission is selected as one fundamental domain. Its high PLQY (up to $50\%$ ）emission (centered at $495\mathsf{n m}$ )could overlaps with the absorption spectrum of the presented TADF MOFs, which is highly suitable as energy donor in ET process.[12] On the other hand, C5 was selected as acceptor domain in consideration of its remarkable PLQY $(7.71\%)$ and its partial absorption cross-section overlap with the emission spectra of Tpl@NKU-111 (Figure 4b). Accordingly, the anisotropic epitaxial growthmethodisutilizedtofabricatetwo heterostructures (H1 and H2) based on distinctive arrangement of C5 and Tpl@NKU-111 domains for optical signal modulation (see supporting information section 2).[11a]
To verify the optical signal tuning performances of the heterostructure crystals, excitation $(405\quad\mathsf{n m})$ at several representative positions $(1^{(\cdot)},\mathsf{J}1^{(\cdot)},2^{(\cdot)},\mathsf{J}2^{(\cdot)},3^{(\cdot)})$ upon H1 and H2 was performed with the PL images (Figure S50 and 4a) and emission spectra (Figure S51 and 4c) recorded at output spot. The optical signals of emission received in output tip upon H1 and H2 are listed (Figure 4d) and summarized, illustrating the obvious and novel light modulation through the heterostructure crystal. As shown in Figure 4a, H2 features C5 as the pristine domain and Tpl@NKU-111 as the extended domain. In regard to the emission obtained by exciting 1' spot, photons are generated from Tpl@NKU-111 and propagated to excited the C5 domain. Then the output signal centered at $532~\mathsf{n m}$ could be attributed to the mixed emission of C5 and Tpl@NKU-111 due to the partial ET. In the case that the heterojunction J1' was excited, photons from both Tpl@NKU-111 and C5 could be generated and propagated along the crystal. Since the ET could not happen from C5 to extended Tpl@NKU-111 domain, the majority of the output signal at the tip should be attributed to C5, which result in the emission band centered at 571 nm. When the C5 domain (2') was excited, only the photons from C5 could be generated due to the absence of ET between domains, which result in the output emission profile peaked at 598 nm. When the heterojunction J2' was excited, the domain of C5 and Tpl@NKU-111 are excited simultaneously, which result in distinctive overlapped optical signals with a maximum of $580~\mathsf{n m}$ and a shoulder peak at $495\ \mathsf{n m}$ .When the extended Tpl@NKU-111 domain was excited, the corresponding photons are produced and propagated to the output tip to give the emission profile peaked at $495\ \mathsf{n m}$ . According to the calculated CIE coordinates of the distinctive emissions (Figure 4e), the output emission color covered a wide range from orange to cyan. More importantly, linear trends could be observed between the orderly scanned excitation positions and the CiE coordinates, indicating the highly tunable nature of the output emissions signals based on the heterostructure crystal H2.
For the heterostructure crystal H1 with Tpl@NKU-111 as the pristine domain and C5 as the extended domain (Figure S50), similar tip output signals (Figure S51)were attained when the crystal was excited at symmetrical positions (1 and 3, J1 and J2). When the 1 or 3 position was excited with $405\mathsf{n m}$ laser, only the emission of C5 (peaked at $598\ \mathsf{n m}$ ）could be detected at the output with the Tpl@NKU-111 domain serve as waveguide.When heterojunction J1 or J2 was excited, the output signal is centered at $580\mathsf{n m}$ , which stems from multiplex emission of Tpl@NKU-111 and C5. The excitation of position 2 result in the output signal centered at $544\mathsf{n m}$ ，which should be attributed to the overlapped emission of Tpl@NKU-111 and the emission of C5 based on ET. The ClE coordinates of the emissions (Figure S52) also reveal linear correlation between the sequential excitation positions and the ClE coordinates but limited tuning range. These results demonstrate that the heterostructure crystals based on TADF MOFs could be rationally designed as photonic modules featuring highly tunable output signals and high efficiency,which could be highly potential for optical integrated microcircuits.
## Conclusion
In summary, aiming at the investigation of new TADF materials and their application for the rationally fabricated of photonic modules for PlCs applications, a new series of D-A MOF featuring TSCT based TADF was constructed based on $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ and derivatives as donor guest and NKU-111 host framework as acceptor. The configuration of $\mathsf{P h-C z}$ and derivatives are found to be regulated into a minor torsion angle by the confined space of the cage structure in NKU-111, which could facilitate the D-A interaction and the corresponding TSCT to promote the TADF. Moreover, through the introducing of -Br groups to the donor, the SOC was successfully enhanced, due to the HAE, to promote the RISC process and the PLQY of the material. Also, the introducing of -Br group was found to be an effective method for the emission wavelength tuning of the system. On the basis of the highly tunable emissions and considerable PLQY of the NKU-111 based TADF MOFs, C5 and Tpl@NKU-111 were rationally integrated into heterostructure crystals for the fabrication of photonic modules. The optimized heterostructure crystals (H2 with Tpl@NKU-111-C5-Tpl@NKU-111 domains） show excitation positions depending tip output optical signals that covered a wide range of wavelength, indicating its potential as highly tunable photonic modules. The achievement of the construction of the new series of TSCT-TADF MOF suggest that D-A MOF could be a highly flexible and potential platform for the development of TADF materials. Accordingly, a variety of D-A MOFs systems featuring distinctive TSCT related properties are expected to be discovered in the future. More importantly, benefiting from the readily integrated D-A MOF domains based on NKU-111 and the abundant optical properties of distinctive D-A systems, heterostructure crystals featuring more complicated optical functions could be expected, which is worth to deeply explore in our further research.
